-- title:
--   Reducedness of the localised weight-two Hecke algebra at θ'
-- statement:
--   Let $\mathcal O$ be a discrete valuation domain of characteristic zero, complete for the adic topology of its maximal ideal and with finite residue field $k=\mathrm{ResidueField}\,\mathcal O$, and let $p$ be a prime with $p\neq 2$ and $p\in\mathfrak m_{\mathcal O}$. Let $\bar\rho$ be a residual Galois representation over $k$, that is, a two-dimensional $k$-vector space $V$ with a monoid homomorphism $\bar\rho.\rho$ from $\mathrm{Gal}(\overline{\mathbb Q}/\mathbb Q)=\mathrm{Aut}_{\mathbb Q}(\overline{\mathbb Q})$ to $\mathrm{End}_k V$ that is trivial on the subgroup fixing some finite extension of $\mathbb Q$ inside $\overline{\mathbb Q}$; assume $\bar\rho$ is absolutely irreducible, i.e. the base change of $\bar\rho$ to $\overline{k}$ has no Galois-stable submodule other than $\bot$ and $\top$. Let $S_0\subseteq S$ be finite sets of primes with $p\in S$, and $N\neq 0$ an integer all of whose prime divisors lie in $S$ and outside $S_0$, with $p^2\nmid N$, subject to the local conditions: $\bar\rho$ is unramified at each $q\in S$ with $q\neq p$, $q\nmid N$ (inertia at every valuation subring of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ in which $q$ is a nonunit acts trivially); for each prime $q\neq p$ with $q\,\|\,N$, $\bar\rho$ is ramified at $q$ and every inertia element at $q$ has characteristic polynomial $(X-1)^2$; for each prime $q\neq p$ with $q^2\mid N$ one has $q^3\nmid N$ and again unipotent inertia at $q$; and if $p\mid N$ then $\bar\rho$ is ordinary at $p$, i.e. above each place over $p$ there is a free rank-one $k$-line spanned by a basis vector, stable under the decomposition group, on whose quotient inertia acts trivially. Assume the weight-two cusp forms on $\Gamma_0(N)$ are spanned over $\mathbb C$ by the $\mathbb Z$-lattice of forms with integral $q$-expansion coefficients, and let $\theta'$ be a ring homomorphism to $k$ from the Hecke algebra $\mathrm{heckeAlgebra}\,N\,2\,S_0$, the $\mathbb Z$-subalgebra of $\mathrm{End}_{\mathbb C}$ of weight-two cusp forms on $\Gamma_0(N)$ generated by the operators $T_\ell$ ($\ell$ prime, $\ell\nmid N$, $\ell\notin S_0$) and $U_q$ ($q$ prime, $q\mid N$, $q\notin S_0$). Suppose $\theta'$ matches $\bar\rho$ in the following sense: for every prime $\ell\neq p$ with $\ell\nmid N$, $\ell\notin S_0$, every valuation subring $P$ of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ lying over $\ell$ and every $\sigma$ that is a Frobenius at $\ell$ for $P$, the characteristic polynomial of $\bar\rho(\sigma)$ equals $X^2-\theta'(T_\ell)X+\ell$; $\theta'(U_q)=0$ whenever $q\mid N$, $q\notin S_0$ and $q^2\mid N$; $\theta'(U_q)$ equals the trace of the endomorphism of the inertia coinvariants $V/\sum_{\tau\in I_P}\mathrm{im}(\bar\rho(\tau)-1)$ induced by a Frobenius $\sigma$ at $q$, whenever $q\mid N$, $q\notin S_0$, $q^2\nmid N$; and, if $p\nmid N$ and $p\notin S_0$, $\theta'(T_p)$ is likewise the trace of induced Frobenius on the inertia coinvariants at $p$. Then the ring $\mathrm{heckeLocal}\,N\,S_0\,\mathcal O\,\theta'$, the localisation of the $\mathcal O$-base change of the integral Hecke lattice at the multiplicative set coming from the complement of the prime ideal cut out by $\theta'$, is reduced.
--
--   This is the reducedness of the localised weight-two Hecke algebra with the $U_q$ included, at the non-Eisenstein maximal ideal attached to $\bar\rho$ and a level whose exponents are exactly as prescribed by the local behaviour of $\bar\rho$; in the Darmon–Diamond–Taylor treatment it underlies the identification of Hecke algebras with deformation rings at levels of type $\Sigma$. It is the hypothesis used by the companion statement [`CuspForm.heckeLocal.exists_points_jointly_injective_of_charpoly_frobenius_eq_of_isAbsolutelyIrreducible`](thm.html#CuspForm.heckeLocal.exists_points_jointly_injective_of_charpoly_frobenius_eq_of_isAbsolutelyIrreducible), which allows identities in the localised Hecke algebra to be tested on its $\mathcal O'$-valued points.
